course: dictionary
lesson: 11045
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Web is one of them. It can mean a spider's trap, the internet, a tangle of connected things, or the skin between a duck's toes. The words around it tell you which meaning is used.
explain: «A garden spider often eats its old web and builds a new one nearly every day.» Spiders make webs, and this web catches insects, so here a web is a net of sticky threads.
explain: «When Ruby types a web address, the browser asks that server for the page.» Typing and browsers tell you this web means the internet. Check by swapping in your meaning: a spider's net does not fit with typing an address.
explain: «Ducks have webbed feet for swimming.» Webbed means joined by skin between the toes, which helps a duck push through water. Always ask which meaning fits the words around it.
fact: A web is a net of thin, sticky threads that a spider makes to catch insects.
fact: The Web, or World Wide Web, is the system of pages and sites on the internet.
fact: A web can also be a complicated network of connected things, like a web of lies or a food web.
fact: Web also means the skin between the toes of ducks, frogs and other swimming animals.
fact: «Darwin's bark spider in Madagascar spins webs across rivers up to about 25 metres wide.»
exercise: «A garden spider often eats its old web and builds a new one nearly every day.» What is a web in this sentence? | A tangle of lies | *A net of thin, sticky threads that catches insects | The skin between a duck's toes | A website with pages to read
exercise: «Ducks have webbed feet for swimming.» What does webbed mean? | Covered in sticky silk | *Joined by skin between the toes | Very wet and muddy | Stuck together with lies
exercise: Ruby types a web address into her browser. What does the Web mean here? | A spider's trap for flies | A food chain in a pond | *The system of pages and sites on the internet | A wet, grey day
test: «A food web shows many food chains joined together.» What does food web mean? | *A network showing who eats whom in a habitat | A cobweb in the kitchen | A spider's silk trap | A web-shaped plate for dinner
test: «Be careful what you share on the web, and tell an adult if you see something upsetting.» Which meaning of web is used? | A tangle of wool | *The internet | A spider's net | The skin between toes
exam: «He told a web of lies to his teacher.» Which meaning of web fits here? | A net of sticky threads for catching flies | The skin between a duck's toes | A web page for homework | *A complicated tangle of connected lies
